WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.469 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:07.540
-Welcome back.
So, when you see...

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.374
This isn't "A Closer Look."

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:10.576
When you see a Ye like this,

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:13.212
it's actually pronounced "The,"

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:15.748
The Olde Diet Coke.

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:18.317
There was a letter called
a thorn.

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.487 line:0%
I don't even know
if I'm holding this right.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:25.125 line:0%
And thorn was the last letter
in the alphabet they let go.

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.325 line:0%
It was --

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:27.960
It basically was "th."

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.129
It made a "th" sound

00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:30.530
and then they took thorn aside
and said,

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.999
"We think we can do it
with 'T' and 'H.'

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:36.269
You're redundant,"
and let it go.

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:39.806
So, but I guess they printed it
with a "Y"

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:41.974
because that was closest
to what it looked like

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:45.145
on the new tyce --
typescript, excuse me.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:50.116
So, when you see a ye,
it actually is the.

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:51.283
The "Y" means "th."

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:52.718
That's very important
to remember.

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:54.487
I guess it's not just
for things like that,

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.089
like that movie,
it's actually pronounced

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:58.825
"Old Theller."

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.627
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.127 --> 00:01:05.598
We also talked about, uh,

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:10.235
Revolutionary War
Founding Father-era Trump

00:01:10.235 --> 00:01:12.705
would have a scam tea.

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:15.475
Because, you know,
they kind of famously were real

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:19.679
into tea back then, you know,
during the Revolutionary War.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.280
You're a history buff,
Shoemaker.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.383
-[ Laughs ]
-And so we had graphics
make that.

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:26.719
I think they made it
a little modern, the box.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.688
[ Laughter ]
The packaging was not

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:34.160
Revolutionary War-style
and somebody wrote, actually,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.197
"Tea at the time would've been
served in a sack."

00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:39.365
And we knew that,

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.534
but when I called
the control booth and said,

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.937
"Can we get the penis tea
on the sack?",

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:45.438
HR cut the line.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:49.309
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.311
Someone says,
"Why do you have a mic here,

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:53.646
if you got a mic on your lapel?"

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.648
This is a backup mic,
in case anything happens

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.083
to the lapel mic.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.284
And this is also interesting.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:01.020
There's a -- [ Grunts ]
There's a speaker...

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.389
See that speaker?
...which is right here

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:07.694
and it's been there
from the very first show.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:09.629
And every night, I trip over it.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.598
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:15.635
Pret-ty cool.

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:19.104
We said that, in the new
Scooby-Doo, Velma is gay.

00:02:19.104 --> 00:02:22.007
Many of you pointed out
she's always been gay.

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:23.042
She's just out now.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.914
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:31.184
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.053
Um --

00:02:35.053 --> 00:02:37.089
Oh, we talked about --

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:40.492
We talked about how sometimes
Fox News graphics

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:44.297
make Joe Biden look really cool

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.466
and they had one of him
wearing sunglasses,

00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:49.302
eating an ice cream cone.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.204
And then we said
that looks like the poster

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.706
of a movie with Jason Statham
called "Ice Cream Man."

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:54.673
Well, guess what, guys.

00:02:54.673 --> 00:02:55.541
There was an actual movie

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:58.010
called "Ice Cream Man,"

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.380
starring one-time "Late Night
with Seth Meyers" guest

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.916
Clint Howard
and here's the poster.

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.418
It was a real movie,
"Ice Cream Man."

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:10.022
And if you're wondering

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:12.158
who did the soundtrack,

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:13.793
it was Randy Newman.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.061
No, it was that guy.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:17.330
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:22.568
In one of our jokes,
we also said, "Somebody, quick,

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:25.671
somebody invent Wite-Out."

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:28.307
Somebody did invent Wite-Out.

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:32.945
Bette Nesmith Graham
invented Wite-Out,

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:34.480
Liquid Paper,
it was called at the time,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.318
history's first correction
fluid, in 1956.

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:43.289
Shoemaker,
do you know who her son was?

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.225
Mike Nesmith from The Monkees.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:48.261
And I knew that Shoemaker
would know that

00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:50.262
because the amount
I've heard him go --

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:52.365
[ As Shoemaker ]
They were my Beatles.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:55.335
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.574 --> 00:04:05.411
Hey, chimpanzees
are not monkeys.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.047
And I guess we said that and I

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:11.550
know that because, back when,
before we had --

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.119
before we stopped having animals
on the show,

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:16.755
we did a segment where

00:04:16.755 --> 00:04:21.460
a chimpanzee handler brought out
a chimp to do a sketch

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:24.230
and the chimp broke free.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.367
And it was
a pretty harrowing day.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:30.102
I hope everybody's okay with me
bringing it back up again.

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:32.538
Tore off down the hallway,
ripping pictures off the wall

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:35.508
and went into our offices
and it was just chaos.

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:37.610
And, fortunately, everybody
kind of got to safety

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.811
and we managed
to close the door,

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:41.480
but you could just
hear him screeching and --

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.682
It was a mess.
So loud.

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:45.217
And, obviously, you know,

00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:47.754
the chimpanzee was so,
you know, out of sorts.

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:49.488
And so we called
New York City Animal Control.

00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:51.057
We said, "Like there's a monkey
loose in our office."

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:52.358
And these guys came,
these two guys,

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.059
pretty quickly, I should note.

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:56.162
Put on these giant,
padded suits.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:59.932
And then they went in
about 15 minutes.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:03.002
And we just heard the monkey --
the chimpanzee screeching.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.737
And then they came out.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.371
Still heard the screeching
in the background,

00:05:06.371 --> 00:05:08.040
like "Aar! Aar! Aar!"

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.845
And they looked at us and said,
"There's no monkey in there."

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.079
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:15.114
And we were like, "What?"
And they go,

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:18.484
"It's a chimpanzee.
They're apes."

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.419
We were like, "Okay, well,
can you get it out?"

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:22.788
And they're like,
"No, because our action order

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:24.190
that you called in...

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.491
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.926
...we put down monkey
and that's --"

00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:30.496
And they said they had to go all
the way back to the office.

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:32.198
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:36.902
We said the world's
oldest dog died.

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:38.504
We were told that's impossible
because,

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.172
[ Snapping fingers ]
the minute It dies,

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:42.141
there's a new
world's oldest dog.

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:48.114
Although, they also wrote,
It's a wrong sentence."

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:52.151
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.386
On that note,
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle --

00:05:54.386 --> 00:05:55.421
we called him that.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.022
We've been told this before.

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:59.559
"You don't take the knighthood
with you to the grave.

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:00.993
So, you actually would,
right now call him

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:02.661
Arthur Conan Doyle."

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:04.831
Although --
and I'm not naming names --

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.532
the person who took it
away from you

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.234
is also dead now, so...

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:10.870
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.672
...I think you could try to keep
from here on out.

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:17.275
We mentioned the Jeffrey Dahmer
show on Netflix.

00:06:17.275 --> 00:06:20.346
I misstated the title.

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:21.747
The actual title is

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:26.519
"Dahmer -- Monster:
The Jeffrey Dahmer Story."

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.321
Because I guess it was
originally called

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:30.623
"Dahmer -- Monster"
and there were some --

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.091
there was like a Glenn Dahmer.

00:06:32.091 --> 00:06:33.492
There were some other Dahmers
out there...

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:34.893
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:37.396
...who also wear
prescription aviators

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.798
and I guess it was

00:06:39.798 --> 00:06:41.033
very awkward for them.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:42.534
They maybe had some connections
at Netflix

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:44.937
and they're like, "Can you
really hammer home it's not --

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:46.873
[ Laughter ]
which Dahmer it's about?"

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:52.778
I said, "A magniflying lens."

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:54.480
That's a flub,
you sons of bitches.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.117
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.117 --> 00:06:59.685
Actually, I shouldn't say that

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:01.253
because I think a lot of you
who said that were ladies.

00:07:01.253 --> 00:07:03.655
That's a flub,

00:07:03.655 --> 00:07:05.458
you sons and daughters
of bitches.

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:07.560
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.330
Hey, we said --
we joked that Donald Trump

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.400
read "Goodnight Moon"
to his kids

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.368
and it was from the
New York City Public Library.

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:17.837
And one of you said
he couldn't have done that

00:07:17.837 --> 00:07:20.105
because the New York City
Public Library

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:23.208
did not have that book
until 1972 because --

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:25.744
this is true --
the librarian in charge

00:07:25.744 --> 00:07:28.213
of the New York City Public
Library did not like that book.

00:07:28.213 --> 00:07:30.882
But I will note that,
as long as it was there in 1972,

00:07:30.882 --> 00:07:33.719
that is in time for Donald Trump
to read it to his kids,

00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:34.653
based on when they were born.

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:36.422
Point is,
he never read it to them.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:38.624
But it is
an interesting story.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:43.028
The reason she banned it --
Because you would think,

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:45.497
"What is the problem
with 'Goodnight Moon'?"

00:07:45.497 --> 00:07:47.700
Is she thought it sucked.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.703
[ Laughter ]

00:07:54.407 --> 00:07:56.508
These are some fun
viewer questions.

00:07:56.508 --> 00:07:57.776
"Just a question.

00:07:57.776 --> 00:07:59.078
Who are the people who laugh?"

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:00.980
The crew.
"Did you hire them?"

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:03.115
I did.
"Why do they laugh?"

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:03.983
I don't know.

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.852
[ Laughter ]

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:09.121
"Who is the dude laughing
out loud in the background

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:10.589
in every 'Corrections' take?

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:13.859
It's adding to the comedic
value somewhat."

00:08:13.859 --> 00:08:16.595
I'll give you a dollar
if you can guess his name.

00:08:16.595 --> 00:08:20.265
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:20.265 --> 00:08:22.400
Or, to put it another way,

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.003
I'll give you a buck.

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:27.006
[ Laughter ]

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:35.047
We do have

00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:37.416
international viewers
and one of them wrote --

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:39.118
because, again, we have been
leaning into it

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.352
the last couple of weeks --

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:43.522
"I still don't know what sport
the Mets play."

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:46.358
[ Laughter ]

00:08:46.358 --> 00:08:48.060
Well, when you figure it out,

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.128
[ Muttering ]
tell the Mets.

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:52.164
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.669 --> 00:08:57.703
Whoo!

00:08:57.703 --> 00:08:59.237
[ Claps, snaps fingers ]
You guys remember

00:08:59.237 --> 00:09:01.073
we gave you a P.O. Box

00:09:01.073 --> 00:09:04.476
to send in postcards
and, well, oh, well,

00:09:04.476 --> 00:09:05.878 line:0%
look at the jackals.

00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:07.212 line:0%
Look what they've done.

00:09:07.212 --> 00:09:10.749
One from Paris, one from Bodo.
I'll guess that's Norway.

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:12.585
This is
the Swedish royal family.

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:13.452
That's pretty exciting.

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:14.286
[ Gasps ]

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:15.888
Harrogate!

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:17.156
Harrow-gait.

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:18.123
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.123 --> 00:09:18.857
Ooh, remember we talked

00:09:18.857 --> 00:09:20.025
about Tallinn, Estonia?

00:09:20.025 --> 00:09:22.194
Well, that's
my Estonia postcard.

00:09:22.194 --> 00:09:23.896
And now, you guys remember

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:26.065
the reason I gave that P.O. Box
is I just said,

00:09:26.065 --> 00:09:27.967
"Do with it what you will."

00:09:27.967 --> 00:09:31.904
I at no point implied
there was a contest

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:35.408
or that I would do a drawing
for you to get a mug.

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:38.010
If I did,
that was clearly facetious...

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.445
[ Laughter ]

00:09:40.445 --> 00:09:42.314
...because, if I had done that.

00:09:42.314 --> 00:09:45.250
based on what I'm hearing
from our legal department,

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.286
I'm in a [bleep] world
of [bleep].

00:09:47.286 --> 00:09:50.255
[ Laughter ]

00:09:50.255 --> 00:09:52.657
Okay? So, look, if you want
to send a postcard

00:09:52.657 --> 00:09:55.527
to the P.O. Box, yeah, do that,

00:09:55.527 --> 00:09:57.163
with no promise from me.

00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:01.199
And maybe, at one point,
I'll be walking down the hallway

00:10:01.199 --> 00:10:04.436
and someone will bump into me
and one will fall, right?

00:10:04.436 --> 00:10:09.541
And I'll just pick it up
and say, "Oh, here's an idea.

00:10:09.541 --> 00:10:11.177
I'll send them a mug."

00:10:11.177 --> 00:10:15.881
But it's not a contest
or a drawing.

00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:17.616
It's not a situation

00:10:17.616 --> 00:10:20.119
where my phone's ringing
off the [bleep] hook...

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:21.654
[ Laughter ]

00:10:21.654 --> 00:10:23.555
...from the legal department,
saying,

00:10:23.555 --> 00:10:25.324
"What the [bleep] did you do?!

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:27.793
[ Laughter ]

00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:29.395
We didn't see this script!"

00:10:29.395 --> 00:10:31.396
And then you have to be like,
"Well, there's not a script

00:10:31.396 --> 00:10:32.497
for 'Corrections.'"
And they're like,

00:10:32.497 --> 00:10:34.266
"What the [bleep]
is 'Corrections'?!"

00:10:34.266 --> 00:10:37.369
[ Laughter ]

00:10:37.369 --> 00:10:39.171
Just mean!

00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:42.274
[ Laughter ]

00:10:44.843 --> 00:10:46.812
I'm so happy
about my postcards, though.

00:10:46.812 --> 00:10:48.914
[ Laughter ]

00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:51.483
Look, if there was to be
a random moment

00:10:51.483 --> 00:10:53.118
where somebody bumped into me

00:10:53.118 --> 00:10:55.287
and I found one
and then that --

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:57.556
because it was cool,
and for no other reason,

00:10:57.556 --> 00:10:58.624
I decided to send a mug,

00:10:58.624 --> 00:11:00.792
we're going to do that
in two weeks.

00:11:00.792 --> 00:11:02.194
[ Laughter ]
I have a feeling

00:11:02.194 --> 00:11:03.963
somebody might bump into me.

00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:06.499
[ Laughter ]

00:11:06.499 --> 00:11:07.633
Uh-uh!

00:11:09.968 --> 00:11:11.136
So, somebody said this --

00:11:11.136 --> 00:11:13.605
you know, again,
we're talking about --

00:11:13.605 --> 00:11:15.975
Oh, sorry, I should say this
real quick.

00:11:15.975 --> 00:11:17.743
Um --

00:11:17.743 --> 00:11:21.547
We did a Michael Myers
from "Halloween" sketch.

00:11:21.547 --> 00:11:23.448
Mike Scollins wrote it.

00:11:23.448 --> 00:11:27.686
He also cast himself as --
the part was Sexy Teen.

00:11:27.686 --> 00:11:30.289
[ Laughter ]

00:11:30.289 --> 00:11:32.024
Now, nobody, I should note,

00:11:32.024 --> 00:11:34.793
nobody was upset
at the Sexy part.

00:11:34.793 --> 00:11:36.262
Like everybody
signed off on that.

00:11:36.262 --> 00:11:38.998
There were some jackals
who thought

00:11:38.998 --> 00:11:41.133
